---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Jargon from '../components/Jargon.astro';
import MaintainerOnly from '../components/MaintainerOnly.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro-expressive-code/components';

const CHECK_PROMPT = `Check my design system against the principles, read-only. Do not rename or write anything.

1. Collect, from my component library (Figma file, code, or both):
   - every prop or variant property name, with its allowed values and the
     components that declare it
   - every token name, grouped by prefix
   - every default value, and every prop that has none
2. Principle 1, one name for one concept: list every concept with more than
   one spelling (large / lg, outline / outlined), with the count of uses of
   each and the components affected.
3. Principle 2, narrow literal types: list every prop typed as a free string
   or number where a closed set of values would do.
4. Principle 3, sensible defaults: list every required prop that has an
   obvious default.
5. Principle 4, composition over configuration: list every component with
   more than one prop that carries markup or content as a string.
6. Rank the findings by how likely they are to make an agent generate wrong
   code. Name the single one that would confuse it most, and say why.
   Do not apply anything.`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="LLM-optimized APIs"
  description="Design principles behind Atelier UI — API shapes that help language models generate correct, idiomatic component code on the first try."
  currentPath="/design-principles"
  tocItems={[
    { id: 'principle-1', label: 'One name for one concept' },
    { id: 'principle-2', label: 'Narrow literal types' },
    { id: 'principle-3', label: 'Sensible defaults' },
    { id: 'principle-4', label: 'Composition over configuration' },
    { id: 'principle-5', label: 'Single reactivity model' },
    { id: 'principle-6', label: 'MCP server closes the loop' },
    { id: 'principle-7', label: 'No design system from scratch' },
    { id: 'context-lifecycle', label: 'The context lifecycle' },
    { id: 'check-your-own', label: 'Exercise: check your own system' },
    { id: 'deprecation', label: 'Deprecation windows and codemods' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="docs-inline-page">
    <PageHero title="LLM-optimized API design" ledeMax="580px">
      <strong>Predictable APIs across frameworks.</strong>
      Traditional component libraries are designed for humans who browse docs,
      copy examples, and tweak. An LLM-optimized library flips the priority: the
      model has to infer correct usage from minimal context and produce working
      code on the first try. The seven principles below are what make that
      possible — and what shaped every API decision in Atelier UI.
    </PageHero>

    <!-- Principle 1 -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="principle-1" class="docs-section-title">
        1. One name for one concept — everywhere
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        If <code>AtlButton</code> takes a <code>variant</code> prop, then
        <code>AtlBadge</code>, <code>AtlAlert</code>, and <code>AtlCard</code>
        use <code>variant</code> too. If <code>size</code> controls scale on a
        button, it controls scale on every component that has scale. No
        synonyms, no renamings.
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        The model only needs to learn the pattern once. Every subsequent
        component is a variation on the same schema — so the inference cost is
        near zero and hallucination risk drops to near zero.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-comparison">
        <div class="docs-comparison-bad">
          <div class="docs-comparison-label">Unpredictable</div>
          <div class="docs-code-block">
            <div class="docs-code-block-header">
              <span class="docs-code-block-lang">jsx</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code>{"// Three components, three different names\n<Button kind=\"filled\" scale=\"lg\" />\n<Badge type=\"success\" />\n<Alert level=\"warning\" />"}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="docs-comparison-good">
          <div class="docs-comparison-label">LLM-optimized</div>
          <div class="docs-code-block">
            <div class="docs-code-block-header">
              <span class="docs-code-block-lang">jsx</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code>{"// One pattern, every component\n<AtlButton variant=\"primary\" size=\"lg\" />\n<AtlBadge variant=\"success\" />\n<AtlAlert variant=\"warning\" />"}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        Every component with variants uses <code>variant</code>. Every component
        with sizes uses <code>size</code>. The rule has no exceptions by design,
        but the library's own scan still finds a few violations, listed under
        <a href="/figma#shared-language" class="docs-link-primary"
          >Shared language</a
        >.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Principle 2 -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="principle-2" class="docs-section-title">
        2. Narrow literal types, not wide strings
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        A prop typed as <code>string</code> gives the model nothing to work
        with. It has to guess valid values from context, documentation, or
        training data — all unreliable. A prop typed as
        <code>'primary' | 'secondary' | 'outline'</code> is self-documenting:
        the model reads the type and knows exactly what to write.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-comparison">
        <div class="docs-comparison-bad">
          <div class="docs-comparison-label">Unpredictable</div>
          <div class="docs-code-block">
            <div class="docs-code-block-header">
              <span class="docs-code-block-lang">ts</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code>{"// What values are valid here?\nvariant: string\nsize: string\nposition: string"}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="docs-comparison-good">
          <div class="docs-comparison-label">LLM-optimized</div>
          <div class="docs-code-block">
            <div class="docs-code-block-header">
              <span class="docs-code-block-lang">ts</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code>{"// Self-documenting — no guessing needed\nvariant: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'outline'\nsize: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg'\nposition: 'top' | 'bottom' | 'left' | 'right'"}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        Use string literal union types everywhere. Never <code>string</code>,
        never numeric codes, never enums — they compile away and disappear from
        type information.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Principle 3 -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="principle-3" class="docs-section-title">
        3. Sensible defaults so bare usage always works
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        Every input has a default.
        <code>&lt;button atl-button&gt;Click&lt;/button&gt;</code> renders a
        correct, styled, accessible button without a single prop. This matters
        for AI output because the model can scaffold something working first and
        add customization in the next turn — instead of needing every prop
        correct on the first attempt.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-comparison">
        <div class="docs-comparison-bad">
          <div class="docs-comparison-label">Unpredictable</div>
          <div class="docs-code-block">
            <div class="docs-code-block-header">
              <span class="docs-code-block-lang">jsx</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code>{"// Crashes or renders nothing without required props\n<Button />\n<Card type=\"?\" padding=\"?\">...</Card>"}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="docs-comparison-good">
          <div class="docs-comparison-label">LLM-optimized</div>
          <div class="docs-code-block">
            <div class="docs-code-block-header">
              <span class="docs-code-block-lang">jsx</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code>{"// Always works — customize only what you need\n<button atl-button>Click</button>\n<atl-card>Content</atl-card>"}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        Every prop must have a sensible default. Required props are only allowed
        when there is no reasonable default (e.g., a data array that must come
        from the consumer).
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Principle 4 -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="principle-4" class="docs-section-title">
        4. Composition over configuration
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        A config object hides its shape from the model — it has to guess which
        keys exist, whether they're required, and how they nest. Composing
        elements doesn't: it's the same HTML-like nesting the model has already
        seen a billion times in training data.
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        Shown here in Angular's own template syntax — the shape is the same in
        every framework, only the composition primitive changes.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-comparison">
        <div class="docs-comparison-bad">
          <div class="docs-comparison-label">Unpredictable</div>
          <div class="docs-code-block">
            <div class="docs-code-block-header">
              <span class="docs-code-block-lang">jsx</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code>{"<!-- Opaque config object — the model must guess the nested shape -->\n<atl-card [config]=\"{ header: { title: '...' }, footer: { actions: [...] } }\" />"}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="docs-comparison-good">
          <div class="docs-comparison-label">LLM-optimized</div>
          <div class="docs-code-block">
            <div class="docs-code-block-header">
              <span class="docs-code-block-lang">jsx</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code>{"<!-- Composition — nesting the model already knows -->\n<atl-card>\n  <atl-card-header>Title</atl-card-header>\n  <atl-card-content>Body text here.</atl-card-content>\n  <atl-card-footer>\n    <button atl-button variant=\"primary\">Save</button>\n  </atl-card-footer>\n</atl-card>"}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          padding-left: 1.5rem;
          display: flex;
          flex-direction: column;
          gap: 0.35rem;
          margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
        "
      >
        <li>
          <strong>Angular:</strong> content projection via
          <code>&lt;ng-content&gt;</code>, named slots via <code>select</code>.
        </li>
        <li><strong>React:</strong> a <code>children</code> prop.</li>
        <li><strong>Vue:</strong> the default <code>&lt;slot /&gt;</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        Never accept a complex config/option object as a component input.
        Compose it from smaller elements instead — structural composition, not
        configuration.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Principle 5 -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="principle-5" class="docs-section-title">
        5. A single reactivity model per framework
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        Mixing patterns forces the model to decide which approach to use — and
        it will sometimes guess wrong. Atelier UI picks one and uses it
        everywhere.
      </p>
      <ul
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          padding-left: 1.5rem;
          display: flex;
          flex-direction: column;
          gap: 0.35rem;
          margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
        "
      >
        <li>
          <strong>Angular:</strong> Signals only —
          <Jargon term="signal input"><code>input()</code></Jargon>,
          <code>output()</code>, <code>model()</code>. No legacy
          <code>@Input</code>/<code>@Output</code> decorators.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>React:</strong> Props + callbacks. No class components, no
          legacy ref patterns.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vue:</strong>
          <Jargon term="composition API">Composition API</Jargon> +
          <code>defineProps</code>/<code>defineEmits</code>. No Options API.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        One mental model per framework. The model learns the pattern from the
        first component and applies it to every other one.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Principle 6 -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="principle-6" class="docs-section-title">
        6. The MCP server closes the loop
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        Even a perfectly designed API has details the model can't infer: exact
        default values, which props interact, what the output event payload
        looks like. The MCP server provides this at runtime — so the model
        queries it instead of guessing.
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        The docs toolset covers the lookup workflow: <code>docs-list</code> for
        discovery, <code>docs-show</code> for the full API of a component, and
        <code>docs-show-story</code> for story-level usage patterns. Together
        they mean the model never has to rely on training data for
        component-specific facts.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        The library design reduces guessing. The MCP server reduces it further.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Principle 7 -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="principle-7" class="docs-section-title">
        7. Don't generate a design system from scratch
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        Prompting "make me a design system" works, technically. What comes back
        is not your organisation's system, because the prompt carries none of
        your organisational reality. A design system is the story of how
        <em>your</em> organisation designs and builds products, and a model
        starting from nothing has none of that story. It is the same lookalike
        problem as adopting someone else's system wholesale: you can never be
        sure it serves your needs, because it was shaped somewhere else.
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        Use AI for the two jobs where it helps: gathering and structuring your
        real context (existing UI, brand, tech stack, past decisions, the
        problems you are trying to solve), and keeping a system that already
        exists consistent. Deciding what the system should be stays a human
        call. This is also why Atelier starts from a Figma master you drew, not
        from a prompt.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        AI gathers context and enforces consistency. People decide what the
        system is.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Context lifecycle -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="context-lifecycle" class="docs-section-title">
        The context lifecycle: who carries the context?
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        The seven principles are about one component's API. The same question
        applies to the path a component takes from Figma to npm. The course
        behind our training calls it the
        <em>context-based design system workflow</em>: each stage of the design
        and development lifecycle inherits the context of the stage before it,
        so quality is preserved and fewer things slip between the cracks
        (transcript 064). The context is strongest at the source of truth, and
        every layer downstream inherits what was applied there (transcript 009).
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        The course's stages are design ideation, design QA and validation, a
        design-to-development protocol over MCP, review of a pull request from a
        design branch, code validation and testing, then publish and versioning
        (transcript 238). Atelier already has an artefact at most of those
        joints. It just never gave the idea a name:
      </p>
      <ol
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin: 0 0 1rem 1.25rem;
        "
      >
        <li>
          <strong>Ideation.</strong> The Figma master on the Components page,
          with its variants and <code>--ui-*</code> token bindings. This is the
          source of truth the rest inherits from.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Design QA.</strong> The review mode of the
          <code>design-to-code</code> skill ("design QA on X"), plus
          <code>check:figma</code> and <code>check:figma-token-names</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Design-to-development protocol.</strong> The
          <a href="/design-to-code" class="docs-link-primary"
            >handoff document</a
          >
          (ADR-0096): scope, token bindings, and the behaviour a picture cannot
          carry, written down before any code is generated. The agent reads the
          master through figma-console-mcp.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Review.</strong> The contract in
          <code>libs/spec/src/contracts/</code> (only the deliberate
          Figma-to-code mismatches) and the repo's agent instructions,
          <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.
          <code>check:contracts</code> joins the contract, the manifest and the
          stories against the Figma snapshot.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tests.</strong> The stories are the claims (ADR-0121): one
          story per variant value and Boolean state, a <code>play</code> per
          behaviour line from the handoff document, every story rendered in
          Chromium and axe-checked by <code>check:stories</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Publish.</strong> The <code>Publish</code> workflow, which
          starts with a verify (release gate) job. Passing local gates says
          nothing about what reached npm.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        <strong>Who owns it?</strong> The course names the role. After the pull
        request, the work lands with a "context engineer", described as more of
        a shepherd who makes sure what was done in the first part of the
        workflow makes its way into the product engineering workflow (transcript
        238). It is someone who applies organisational standards to a draft that
        a product designer made, usually with AI doing part of the review. The
        speaker in 009 adds that everyone on their team plays that role in some
        way on a design system project. Atelier does not staff the role
        separately. Whoever writes the handoff document owns the context at the
        start, and from there the contract, the stories and the gates are what
        carry it, so it does not live in one person's head. The course also has
        a designer-owned design branch and a prompt-and-iterate playground after
        publish. This repo has no counterpart for either.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        Write the context down at the stage where it is decided, in an artefact
        the next stage reads. If it lives only in someone's head, it stops at
        the handoff.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Exercise -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="check-your-own" class="docs-section-title">
        Exercise: check your own system
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        The principles are only useful if you can hold your own library against
        them. This prompt is the read-only consistency report from
        <a href="/figma" class="docs-link-primary">Figma</a>, widened to cover
        principles 1 to 4. Run it in your agent against your own file or code.
      </p>
      <Code code={CHECK_PROMPT} lang="text" title="Principles check prompt" />
      <ol
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin: 1rem 0 1rem 1.25rem;
        "
      >
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">Pick one.</strong> Read
          the ranked list and choose the one finding that most confuses an
          agent. Ignore the rest for now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">Fix it.</strong> Settle on
          one spelling or one closed set of values and change it where it is
          declared.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">Re-run.</strong> Run the
          same prompt again. The finding should be gone and nothing new should
          have appeared.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        Timebox: 30 minutes. If the first run takes longer than 10, scope it to
        one component family and widen it later.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        One finding fixed and re-checked beats a long report nobody acts on.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Deprecation -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="deprecation" class="docs-section-title">
        Deprecation windows and codemods
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        Consistent names only pay off if you can change them later. The course
        warns that the normalising pass of its shared-language station can upend
        hard-earned naming conventions and break things in production, so be
        careful with component props, values and token names that consumers
        already use. Its answer is version control, codemods and good
        communication (transcript 213). A rename is a migration for everyone
        downstream, and your agents are among them.
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        <strong>The pattern.</strong> The course demo renames
        <code>inverted</code> to <code>knockout</code>, keeping the old prop as
        a deprecated alias with a recorded removal date (see
        <a href="/tokens#naming" class="docs-link-primary">Tokens, naming</a>).
        The more consequential and breaking the change, the longer the window
        (transcript 209).
      </p>
      <div class="docs-code-block">
        <div class="docs-code-block-header">
          <span class="docs-code-block-lang">ts</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code>{"// Sketch for your own design system, after the pattern in transcript 209\n/** Renders for a dark surface. */\nknockout?: boolean;\n/** @deprecated Use `knockout`. Removed after the deprecation window. */\ninverted?: boolean;\n\nconst isKnockout = knockout || inverted; // old callers keep working"}</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin: 0.75rem 0;
        "
      >
        <strong>How an agent learns it.</strong> In the demo the presenter asks
        the agent to confirm that the new name is the default way of expressing
        the use case in the design system's MCP, and that the old one still
        works but is flagged deprecated (transcript 209). The agent answers that
        it is exposed there. Without that, an agent keeps generating the old
        prop, because that is what the existing code and docs show.
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        <strong>Codemods</strong> are the mechanical half: a script that
        rewrites consumer source from the old shape to the new one, so a
        deprecation window ends in a migration rather than a surprise. The
        course names them but does not demonstrate one (transcript 213).
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        "
      >
        <strong>Figma side.</strong> The <code>figma-workspace-architect</code>
        skill's migration playbook has the same shape for a design file: add the
        new shape alongside the old, mark the old one deprecated (a
        <code>_deprecated</code> prefix or an updated description), migrate
        consumers, wait until the next design review or code release, whichever
        is later, then remove. Its warning: if you want to skip the wait because
        "nobody uses it", assume someone does. Variant properties have no alias
        mechanism, so a variant rename always takes this route.
      </p>
      <MaintainerOnly
        label="Release history and this repo's deprecation state · local only"
      >
        <p
          style="
            color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            line-height: 1.65;
            margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
          "
        >
          <strong>What Atelier has today.</strong> Less than the pattern above.
          Releases use <code>nx release</code> with conventional commits for the
          library packages. Below 1.0 a <code>feat</code> rolls the patch
          version and a <code>!</code> (a breaking change) the minor: ADR-0163
          below took the packages from 0.3 to 0.4. While the packages are below
          1.0, ADR-0023 says there is no API-stability promise and breaking
          changes are allowed in any release. It reserves 1.0.0 for an explicit
          decision, paired with an API-stability and deprecation policy that the
          audit found missing. That policy is still unwritten: there is no
          <code>@deprecated</code> tag in the library code, and no codemod in
          the repo.
        </p>
        <p
          style="
            color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            line-height: 1.65;
            margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
          "
        >
          <strong>A worked example of the tradeoff.</strong> ADR-0163 changed
          the Angular button from <code>&lt;atl-button&gt;</code> to
          <code>&lt;button atl-button&gt;</code>, because the custom element
          could not submit a form or be reached with the keyboard. It shipped as
          a <code>feat(angular)!:</code> commit with no deprecation window and
          no codemod, and the ADR records it as breaking for Angular consumers.
          The ADR does not claim a window was considered. It rejects keeping
          <code>&lt;atl-button&gt;</code> around an inner
          <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> because tooltip, menu trigger and
          <code>aria-label</code> would all need forwarding. In a pre-1.0
          library that is a defensible call. In a design system other teams
          build on, the same rename would want a window, a migration script, and
          an announcement first.
        </p>
      </MaintainerOnly>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.65;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        <strong>Does the manifest carry the tag?</strong> Partly verified. The
        Angular docgen worker in <code>@storybook/angular-vite</code> reads a
        <code>@deprecated</code> JSDoc tag into its member data and writes it
        back out in the doc comment it generates. We did not confirm that the
        Storybook MCP's <code>docs-show</code> prints it: the prop mapping in
        <code>@storybook/mcp</code> passes the prop description along, and we
        found no separate deprecated field there. Nothing in this repo is marked
        deprecated, so there is no example to test against. For your own system,
        put the replacement and the removal date in the description text, so it
        reaches the agent even if the tag does not, then test it by asking the
        MCP about the old prop.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-rule">
        <span class="docs-rule-label">Rule</span>
        Add the new name next to the old one, flag the old one deprecated with a
        removal date, and check that the agent's source of truth shows the flag.
        Remove only after the window, and give the consumers a script.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

